## Layout

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `{{.EntrypointFile}}` | Runtime entrypoint. The message handler is an echo placeholder; replace it with the agent logic. |
| `runtime.yaml` | Runtime definition (§5.1). Declares `integrationLevel: {{.IntegrationLevel}}`. |
| `Dockerfile` | Container image build. |
| `Makefile` | `build`, `test`, and `push` targets. |
| `.github/workflows/ci.yml` | CI workflow that builds, tests, and publishes the image. |

## Build and test

```
make build IMAGE={{.Image}}
make test
```

## Validate

`lenny runtime validate` checks the repository against the Runtime
Adapter Specification (§15.4) and the `runtime.yaml` contract:

```
lenny runtime validate
```

## Publish

`lenny runtime publish` pushes the image and registers the runtime with
a gateway. It requires `LENNY_API_URL` and an admin token:

```
make push IMAGE={{.Image}}
lenny runtime publish {{.Name}} --image {{.Image}}
```

## Becoming a reference runtime

The §26.12 "Reference Runtime Proposal Process" describes the path a
community-authored runtime takes to join the first-party reference
slate. Proposals are submitted to `github.com/lennylabs/runtime-templates`
with three artifacts:

- (a) The scaffolded runtime from `lenny runtime init` (this directory).
- (b) Conformance test results at the claimed level, produced by
  `lenny runtime validate` against the §15.4.6 conformance test suite.
- (c) An appendix entry for the reference catalog, reviewed by Lenny
  maintainers.

See [`spec/26_reference-runtime-catalog.md`](https://github.com/lennylabs/lenny/blob/main/spec/26_reference-runtime-catalog.md)
§26.12 for the current process. Community runtimes that do not seek
reference status publish directly via the steps above without filing
a §26.12 proposal.
